 as 2.68 cents according to advocacy  group the garment workers center  director marissa nuncio at those low  rates they can never  reach the minimum wage it's just it's  impossible even the most seasoned  skilled workers  cannot sow fast enough how fast would  they have to work to make the la minimum  wage of fifteen dollars an hour a worker  paid five cents per piece would have to  sow 300 pieces in an hour  it's really fizzy for me it's very  difficult says this worker ver hilda who  only wanted to be identified by her  first name because she fears retaliation  she's worked in the garment shops for  over 20 years but says she still earns  less than five dollars an hour  if you ask for a pay raise she told us  they say if you want to work then work  if not there's the door  we wanted to see the conditions for  ourselves so we went with francisco zul  to a building where he used to work  visiting a different factory you are the  owner  inside no air conditioning as workers  sewed or trimmed stacks of inexpensive  looking clothes  we asked shop owner valentin carbajal  how much would someone get paid to make  that but the the company paid me maybe  around seven fifty seven  oh seventy five so the company pays you  around seven dollars and fifty cents for  this garment  carbajal told us with brands paying so  little for the garments he can't afford  to pay his workers much are you saying  that you can't raise their salaries  because  you're not getting paid enough  this employee maria told us she makes  200 a week so you're saying sometimes at  the end of the week you don't even have  the cash to pay the workers  yeah  that's why last month francisco zul and  other garment workers rallied in  california's capital to push legislation  to make brand name clothing companies  who control pricing not just the  subcontractors subject to increased  liability if workers aren't paid what  they're owed we have to make the  liability go to the brands state senator  maria elena 